[{:db/id 17592186184196 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6d3e3740-f421-5f05-912d-ea42f86d4dd7" "question-836e7d2e-0ac3-5b0c-b2c3-d01db384e22c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184094 :question/problem "Let $V$ be the set of all real polynomials of degree exactly $3$, equipped with the usual addition and scalar multiplication operations. Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$V$ is **not** a vector space because $\\textrm{_________}\\,.$*\n\nI. the sum of two polynomials of degree $3$ may not be a polynomial of degree $3$\n\nII. $V$ does not contain the polynomial $-p(x)$ for every $p(x) \\in V$\n\nIII. addition of polynomials is not commutative" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184167 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2473766c-4484-58b4-94f0-d8e70773f841"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-6d3e3740-f421-5f05-912d-ea42f86d4dd7" :question/id #uuid "6d3e3740-f421-5f05-912d-ea42f86d4dd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-106816" :question/problem "Let $P$ be the set of all ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that $xy=0.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$P$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to the usual component-wise addition and scalar multiplication in ${R}^{2}$) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$P$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to the usual component-wise addition and scalar multiplication in ${R}^{2}$) because **addition of ordered pairs in $P$ is not closed.** *  \n\nFor example, $(1,0)\\in P$ because $1\\cdot 0=0$ and $(0,1)\\in P$ because $0\\cdot 1=0.$ However, if we add the two pairs component-wise, we get\n\n$$\n(1,0)+(0,1)=(1+0,0+1)=(1,1).\n$$\n\nThe product of the components of this pair is $1\\cdot 1=1\\ne 0.$ So, $(1,0)+(0,1)\\notin P.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Multiplication of $(x,y)\\in P$ by a scalar is closed. Indeed, if $\\alpha \\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\alpha \\cdot (x,y)=(\\alpha x,\\alpha y)\\in P\n$$\n\nsince $(\\alpha x)\\cdot (\\alpha y)={\\alpha }^{2}{{(xy)}_{⏟}}_{=0}=0.$\n$\\,✓$\n\n- The zero element in $P$ is $(0,0).$ $✓$\n\n- For an ordered pair $(x,y)\\in P,$ the ordered pair $-(x,y)=(-x,-y)$ is also an element of $P$ since $(-x)\\cdot (-y)=xy=0.$\n$\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot (x,y)=(x,y)$ for all $(x,y)\\in P.$ $\\,✓$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-657d2f5e-8283-512c-8736-a6b6bb9a9cf9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-657d2f5e-8283-512c-8736-a6b6bb9a9cf9" :answer-field/id #uuid "657d2f5e-8283-512c-8736-a6b6bb9a9cf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a00c70d5-ced0-52ad-b2b1-04b1b14b6676" :answer-field/choices ["answer-42ef8948-2af5-570c-9086-ec884dd8b607" "answer-d2e42310-8e15-5635-82fb-c6e17cfba1a0" "answer-a00c70d5-ced0-52ad-b2b1-04b1b14b6676" "answer-cc589644-69c0-5cf3-a95d-7450d4d2431d" "answer-6a2c8125-7050-51a6-849e-7893b298fda5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42ef8948-2af5-570c-9086-ec884dd8b607" :answer/id #uuid "42ef8948-2af5-570c-9086-ec884dd8b607" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1\\cdot (x,y)\\ne (x,y)$ for some $(x,y)\\in P$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2e42310-8e15-5635-82fb-c6e17cfba1a0" :answer/id #uuid "d2e42310-8e15-5635-82fb-c6e17cfba1a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P$ does not contain the zero element" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a00c70d5-ced0-52ad-b2b1-04b1b14b6676" :answer/id #uuid "a00c70d5-ced0-52ad-b2b1-04b1b14b6676" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "addition of ordered pairs in $P$ is not closed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc589644-69c0-5cf3-a95d-7450d4d2431d" :answer/id #uuid "cc589644-69c0-5cf3-a95d-7450d4d2431d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "multiplication by a scalar is not closed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a2c8125-7050-51a6-849e-7893b298fda5" :answer/id #uuid "6a2c8125-7050-51a6-849e-7893b298fda5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "there exists an ordered pair $(x,y)\\in P$ such that $(x,y)+({x}^{′},{y}^{′})\\ne (0,0)$ for any $({x}^{′},{y}^{′})\\in P$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-836e7d2e-0ac3-5b0c-b2c3-d01db384e22c" :question/id #uuid "836e7d2e-0ac3-5b0c-b2c3-d01db384e22c" :question/math-academy-id "q-43858" :question/problem "Let $V$ be the set of all  $2\\times 2$ invertible matrices with real entries, with the usual matrix addition and scalar multiplication. Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$V$ is **not** a vector space  because $\\text{____}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is \n\n*$V$ is **not** a vector space  because **the $2\\times 2$ zero matrix is not in $V.$** *\n\nIndeed, the zero matrix doesn't have an inverse, so it doesn't belong to $V.$ As a result, $V$ cannot be a vector space.\n\nOn the other hand, \n\n- Addition of matrices is associative.  $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of matrices is commutative.  $\\,✓$\n\n- The $2\\times 2$ identity matrix is invertible, so it belongs to $V.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- If $A$ is invertible then $-A$ is invertible too. So, $-A\\in V.$ $\\,✓$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c1c519c6-ee9a-53ca-a030-5c374bc6f933"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c1c519c6-ee9a-53ca-a030-5c374bc6f933" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c519c6-ee9a-53ca-a030-5c374bc6f933" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b58a38fd-ec79-5101-86b4-974746234698" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0cf95fce-46b3-5fe1-926b-157ab6915f04" "answer-11142ab4-ada0-51aa-ab0b-0e0d13397eb2" "answer-7d8749ff-5a89-5515-91b1-92f223a41f80" "answer-c34ae4c7-6893-5a03-b0ce-39213abac829" "answer-b58a38fd-ec79-5101-86b4-974746234698"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0cf95fce-46b3-5fe1-926b-157ab6915f04" :answer/id #uuid "0cf95fce-46b3-5fe1-926b-157ab6915f04" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "If $A\\in V$ then $-A\\notin V$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11142ab4-ada0-51aa-ab0b-0e0d13397eb2" :answer/id #uuid "11142ab4-ada0-51aa-ab0b-0e0d13397eb2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "addition of matrices in $V$ is not associative" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d8749ff-5a89-5515-91b1-92f223a41f80" :answer/id #uuid "7d8749ff-5a89-5515-91b1-92f223a41f80" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "addition of matrices in $V$ is not commutative" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c34ae4c7-6893-5a03-b0ce-39213abac829" :answer/id #uuid "c34ae4c7-6893-5a03-b0ce-39213abac829" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix  is not in $V$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b58a38fd-ec79-5101-86b4-974746234698" :answer/id #uuid "b58a38fd-ec79-5101-86b4-974746234698" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the $2\\times 2$ zero matrix  is not in $V$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2473766c-4484-58b4-94f0-d8e70773f841" :question/id #uuid "2473766c-4484-58b4-94f0-d8e70773f841" :question/math-academy-id "q-35629" :question/problem "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*$\\alpha 0=0$ for any $\\alpha \\in R$*\n\n*Proof:* \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha 0 & =\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0 & =\\alpha (0+0) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0 & =\\alpha 0+\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0+(-\\alpha 0) & =(\\alpha 0+\\alpha 0)+(-\\alpha 0) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-\\alpha 0\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0+(-\\alpha 0) & =\\alpha 0+(\\alpha 0+(-\\alpha 0)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =\\alpha 0+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ 0 & =\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha 0 & =\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0 & =\\alpha (0+0) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0 & =\\alpha 0+\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A8}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0-\\alpha 0 & =(\\alpha 0+\\alpha 0)-\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{add }-\\alpha 0\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0-\\alpha 0 & =\\alpha 0+(\\alpha 0-\\alpha 0) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =\\alpha 0+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A4}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-700b38c9-75a1-52e4-bc40-89a679395057"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-700b38c9-75a1-52e4-bc40-89a679395057" :answer-field/id #uuid "700b38c9-75a1-52e4-bc40-89a679395057" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3a4204de-6303-5664-ae87-4bb545b11579" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d1440eed-dbe7-537c-9d9e-09e10d43a8a0" "answer-d0cc0d55-4b7a-527b-8c30-4752964eae4f" "answer-2fb37ed3-36ac-5e3f-851e-4f837d79e1fc" "answer-bd7ca824-8d41-582d-8e53-440a67967f94" "answer-3a4204de-6303-5664-ae87-4bb545b11579"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1440eed-dbe7-537c-9d9e-09e10d43a8a0" :answer/id #uuid "d1440eed-dbe7-537c-9d9e-09e10d43a8a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\text{A4}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0cc0d55-4b7a-527b-8c30-4752964eae4f" :answer/id #uuid "d0cc0d55-4b7a-527b-8c30-4752964eae4f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\text{A5}$, $\\text{A2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fb37ed3-36ac-5e3f-851e-4f837d79e1fc" :answer/id #uuid "2fb37ed3-36ac-5e3f-851e-4f837d79e1fc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\text{A6}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd7ca824-8d41-582d-8e53-440a67967f94" :answer/id #uuid "bd7ca824-8d41-582d-8e53-440a67967f94" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\text{A7}$, $\\text{A1}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a4204de-6303-5664-ae87-4bb545b11579" :answer/id #uuid "3a4204de-6303-5664-ae87-4bb545b11579" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\text{A8}$, $\\text{A4}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186185317 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we've seen how some sets equipped with addition and scalar multiplication operations exhibit properties similar to $\\mathbb R^n.$ \n\nIn this lesson, we will formally define what we mean by a **vector space.**\n\nFirst, let $V$ be a set equipped with the following two operations:\n\nVector addition: \n\nFor any two elements $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in V,$ there is a unique element $\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}$ that also lies in $V.$ We say that $V$ is **closed under vector addition.**\n\nScalar multiplication: \n\nFor any element $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$ and any number $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R},$ there is a unique element $\\alpha \\mathbf{x}$ that also lies in $V.$ We say that $V$ is **closed under scalar multiplication.**\n\nA set $V$ equipped with vector addition and scalar multiplication forms a **vector space** if it satisfies the following **vector space axioms**. \n\nAxioms of Vector Addition ($+$)\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A1]}$: $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y} =  \\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x}$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in V$; vector addition is **commutative**.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A2]}$: $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z} =  \\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}, \\mathbf{z} \\in V$; vectors addition is **associative**.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A3]}$: There exists a **zero-vector** $\\mathbf{0}$ such that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{x}$.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A4]}$: For every $\\mathbf{x} \\in V,$ there exists $-\\mathbf{x} \\in V$ such that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x} + (-\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{0}$; $-\\mathbf{x}$ is called the **negative** of $\\mathbf{x}$.\n\nAxioms of Scalar Multiplication ($\\cdot$)\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A5]}$: $\\color{blue}1 \\cdot \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}$ for any $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$; multiplying by $1$ does not change a vector.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A6]}$: $\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{x}$ for any numbers $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and any vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$.\n\nAxioms Connecting Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication (the Distributive Laws)\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A7]}$: $\\color{blue}(\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{x} = \\alpha \\mathbf{x} + \\beta \\mathbf{x}$ for any numbers $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and any vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A8]}$: $\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\mathbf{x} + \\mathbf{y}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{x} + \\alpha \\mathbf{y}$ for any number $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in V$.\n\nSo, a vector space is simply a collective term for all kinds of sets equipped with addition and scalar multiplication, where these operations satisfy this particular list of axioms (which are similar to the properties of these operations in $\\mathbb{R}^n$). \n\nThe elements of any vector space are called **vectors**." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184420 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184930 :step/math-academy-id 653101 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186185014 :step/math-academy-id 653102 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184969 :step/math-academy-id 653103 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184931 :step/math-academy-id 653106 :db/ensure [:step/validate]}]
